@deva[ह्ण] @[h-ṇa], @deva[ह्न] @[h-na], @deva[ह्म] @[h-ma], @deva[र्ह] @[r-ha], @deva[ह्र] @[h-ra], @deva[ह्ल] @[h-la], @deva[ह्व] @[h-va].

Sometimes five consonants are found in conjunction: e.g. @deva[र्त्स्न्य] @[r-t-s-n-ya], as in @deva[कार्त्स्न्य].

@deva[ह्] coming after any of the first four letters of a class is optionally changed to the soft aspirate (4th letter) of that class[^1]; @deva[वाक्] + @deva[हरिः] = @deva[वाग्हरिः] (see b. under @ref[22]), and by this @deva[वाग्घरिः] (@deva[वाचां हरिः]) the possessor of speech, Bṛhaspati.

---

[^1]: @deva[झयो होऽन्यतरस्याम्] । Pāṇ. VIII. 4. 62.

@deva[श्] preceded by a word ending in any of the first four letters of a class and followed by a vowel, a semi-vowel, a nasal or @deva[ह्], is optionally changed to @deva[छ्];[^1] e. g. @deva[तद्] + @deva[शिवः] = @deva[तद्] + @deva[शिवः]; and @deva[तद्] + @deva[छिवः]. and then @deva[तत्] + @deva[शिवः], and @deva[तत्] + @deva[छिवः] by the above, and finally @deva[तच्शिवः] and @deva[तच्छिवः] that Śiva (see a. @ref[28].). Similarly @deva[तच्श्लोकेन], @deva[तच्छ्लोकेन]; but @deva[वाक्श्चोतति] speech falters (as @deva[श] is not followed by any of the letters mentioned in the rule).

---

[^1]: @deva[शश्छोऽटि । खरि च ।] Pāṇ. VI. I. 4. 63, 55.

@deva[च्] is necessarily inserted between @deva[छ्] and the preceding vowel, short or long; and optionally when a long vowel at the end of a word, except that of the particle @deva[मा] and the preposition @deva[आ], precedes it;[^1] e. g. @deva[शिव] + @deva[छाया] = @deva[शिवच्छाया] Śiva's shadow; so @deva[स्वच्छाया]; @deva[चेच्छियते] what is cut again and again; @deva[लक्ष्मी] + @deva[छाया] = @deva[लक्ष्मीछाया] or @deva[लक्ष्मीच्छाया]; @deva[मा] + @deva[छिदत्] = @deva[माच्छिदत्] may he not cut; @deva[आ] + @deva[छादयति] = @deva[आच्छादयति] he covers.

---

[^1]: @deva[छे च]। Pāṇ. VI. 1. 73. Properly @deva[त्] is inserted, but it is changed to @deva[च्] by @ref[28]. @deva[आङ्माङोश्च। दीर्घात्। पदान्ताद्वा]। Pāṇ. VI. 1. 74-76.

The following couplet (Kārikā) gives a rule for the correct use of these pronouns:—

@deva[इदमस्तु सन्निकृष्टं समीपतरवर्ति चैतदो रूपम् ।]
@deva[अदसस्तु विप्रकृष्टं तदिति परोक्षे विजानीयात् ॥]

@deva[इदम्] is used with reference to a person or thing near at hand, and @deva[एतद्] to one nearer still; @deva[अदस्] is used of a person or thing at a distance, while @deva[तद्] is used of one that is absent.

@deva[स्व] when it does not mean "a class" or "wealth" is a pronoun and is declined like @deva[सर्व][^1] m. f. n.; but it forms its Nom plu. and Ab. and Loc. sing. optionally like @deva[राम] when mas.; as @deva[स्वे] or @deva[स्वाः] 'one's own' Nom plu.; but @deva[स्वाः] 'people of one's class' only (declined like @deva[राम]).

---

[^1]: @deva[स्वमज्ञातिधनाख्यायाम् ।] Pāṇ. 1. 1. 35.

@deva[अन्तर] is a pronoun (and therefore declined like @deva[सर्व] m. n. f.) when it means "exterior, outer; or what is to be worn out-side, as a garment"[^1] except when followed by @deva[पुर]; it is optionally declined like nouns ending in @deva[अ] in the Nom. pl. and Ab. and Loc. sing. of the mas. e. g. @deva[अन्तरे अन्तरा वा गृहाः]; @deva[अन्तरे-अन्तरा वा शाटकाः] (garments). But @deva[अन्तरायां पुरि].

